<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Grand Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> was a large state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> that expanded across present-day Lithuania, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> in the fourteenth century. United with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in a personal union and then by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin">Union of Lublin</a> in 1569, it formed one half of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania.jpg" alt="Grand Duchy of Lithuania" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_banner_of_the_Grand_Duchy_of_Lithuania.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its founder, Haci I Giray, a descendant of Genghis Khan through Jochi’s son Tuqa-Timur, had spent years in exile in the <b>Grand Duchy of Lithuania</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Wars with the <b>Grand Duchy of Lithuania</b> in 1487–1494 and 1500–1503, including the victory at the Battle of Vedrosha in 1500, won the Chernigov and Seversk lands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Novgorod tried to balance between Moscow and the <b>Grand Duchy of Lithuania</b>, sometimes inviting Lithuanian princes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/novgorod-republic">Novgorod Republic · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…Union of Lublin on 1 July 1569, a constitutional act that joined the Kingdom of Poland and the <b>Grand Duchy of Lithuania</b> into a single federal state with a common monarch and parliament, one of the largest polities in Europe, covering some 815,000 square kilometres at its formation…</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The modern Republic of Lithuania claims descent from the medieval <b>Grand Duchy of Lithuania</b>, which had shared a Commonwealth with Poland until the partitions of 1772–1795 brought most Lithuanian lands under Russian rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Republic of Lithuania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After a month-long siege, the Teutonic Order captured and destroyed Kaunas Castle, the first brick castle of the <b>Grand Duchy of Lithuania</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-17">17 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
